Terms|

schema drift - different documents might use different field names for the same concept. (Like "Total" vs "Amount Due" etc)

configuration tables - These configurations ensure that the extracted data are correctly structured for further
processing

unit conversion problem - The system needs to understand not just that "500 kg" and "1,102 lbs" both represent weights, but that they're roughly equivalent values that should be standardized for downstream processing.

prompt optimization - to handle schema drift 

domain knowledge problem - Business documents are full of shorthand and conventions that are obvious to humans but opaque to machines. 

Optical Character Recognition (OCR) systems - convert images to text, losing all the spatial and structural information that makes documents meaningful.

Textract - OCR that preserves the relationships between different elements on a page. It can identify that certain text appears in a table, that some fields are associated with specific labels, and that the document has an overall hierarchical structure. 
This structural understanding becomes crucial when dealing with complex business documents where the same piece of information might appear in different locations depending on the document format.

Manual prompt engineering - This is the traditional approach, where human experts craft prompts based on their understanding of the task and the documents. The prompts can include instructions for handling edge cases, format conversions, and missing information. 
While manual prompts can achieve good performance, they have obvious scalability limitations. Each new document type or business requirement might necessitate rewriting significant portions of the prompt. Moreover, manual prompt engineering requires deep expertise in both the domain and the capabilities of the language model, 
making it difficult for non-experts to optimize.

Automatic Prompt Engineering, or APE, treats prompt optimization as a machine learning problem in its own right. The idea is that we can use language models not just to process documents, but also to generate and refine the prompts that guide document processing. 
APE starts with a basic description of the task and some example inputs and outputs, then uses the language model to generate multiple candidate prompts. Each candidate is evaluated on a subset of training data, and the best performers are used as seeds for generating even better prompts. 
This approach has several advantages over manual prompting. It can explore variations that humans might not think of, it can optimize for specific metrics that matter for the application, and it can adapt to new document types without requiring expert human intervention. 
The APE process essentially automates the trial-and-error refinement that human prompt engineers do manually.

Instruction Prompt Calibration, or IPC - is a hybrid approach that tries to combine the best of human expertise and automated optimization. IPC recognizes that while automated systems can efficiently explore large spaces of possible prompts, human judgment is still valuable for assessing qualities like clarity, robustness, and alignment with business requirements. 
In IPC, both human annotators and the language model evaluate each candidate prompt, and the final selection criteria combine feedback from both assessments. By incorporating human judgment into the optimization loop, IPC can identify prompts that are not just high-performing but also robust and interpretable.
